WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.010
Hi everyone, and welcome to Biology Professor.&nbsp;
Today, we're talking about the four different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:11.640
kinds of hypersensitivities. So first, what is&nbsp;
a hypersensitivity? Well, a hypersensitivity is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:20.850
an undesirable or an actually harmful&nbsp;
reaction of a person's immune system.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:33.480
Sometimes also called an intolerance, specifically&nbsp;
when we're talking about sort of more undesirable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.890
things rather than harmful things, but an&nbsp;
intolerance can be used to discuss all of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.720
So now let's talk about those four different types&nbsp;
starting with type 1. Type 1 hypersensitivities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:54.240
are when a specific antibody, specifically IgE,&nbsp;
recognizes soluble antigen. Remember here that Ig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.010
stands for immunoglobulin which is another&nbsp;
name for antibody, IgE is one of the major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:05.340
types of antibodies. So IgE recognizes soluble&nbsp;
antigen. What does this mean? An antigen is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:11.310
the thing being recognized by the immune system.&nbsp;
In this case, it is something soluble, so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:17.400
not something that is cellular or at the tissue&nbsp;
level, it's recognizing just something that's kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.590
of dissolved and floating around in the body.&nbsp;
In particular with type 1 hypersensitivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:32.820
IgE is recognizing things like pet dander, mold,&nbsp;
pollen... things like that that we think of as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:39.180
being related to seasonal allergies. And so IgE&nbsp;
binds to that soluble antigen when it finds it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:46.710
This triggers an immune system cell called mast&nbsp;
cells to do something called to degranulate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:54.930
So the mast cells degranulate. And all that&nbsp;
means is they basically just spill out their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.930 --> 00:02:03.450
guts. These mast cells are full of signaling&nbsp;
compounds that are then just released into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:08.700
the body. And the major one here that we're&nbsp;
concerned with is something called histamine.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:21.680
You might recognize that word because it is&nbsp;
antihistamines that we take when we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:28.580
relieve allergy symptoms. And that's because&nbsp;
histamine release causes the allergy symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.530
that we associate with a lot of these seasonal&nbsp;
allergies or pet allergies. So I'm talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:40.250
coughing, sneezing, red eyes, watery eyes,&nbsp;
hives... those are all being caused by this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:45.170
massive histamine release in the body and&nbsp;
antihistamines can help to control those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:51.560
symptoms. So that is type 1 hypersensitivity.&nbsp;
Now let's talk about type 2. Type 2 is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:57.740
mediated by antibodies but by different kinds of&nbsp;
antibodies. Rather than IgE, the antibodies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:09.590
are in these type 2 hypersensitivities&nbsp;
are IgG and IgM. And they recognize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:14.810
rather than the soluble antigen, stuff just&nbsp;
happening to be floating through the body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:20.810
they're recognizing cellular antigens. So&nbsp;
antigens that are actually on the surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:30.110
of the host cells. And what this does is when&nbsp;
these antibodies recognize and bind to cellular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:36.620
antigens they're essentially binding to cells and&nbsp;
causing a lot of damage to those cells. This can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:46.370
be termed "cyto" (cyto for cell) "toxicity". So&nbsp;
cytotoxicity is when cells are being damaged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:52.790
Some examples of type 2 hypersensitivity are&nbsp;
the recognition of certain self-antigens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:57.950
Self-antigens means something that is self,&nbsp;
something that belongs to the host, that is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:05.390
a foreign entity and this results in conditions&nbsp;
known as autoimmune disorders or autoimmunity.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:18.620
So some of these type 2 hypersensitivities, where&nbsp;
you have IgG and IgM, recognizing these cellular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:26.540
antigens and causing damage to the cells, this&nbsp;
can be sort of self cells, and it can also in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:34.370
particular be red blood cells. So the ABO groups,&nbsp;
if you've heard like Type A, Type B, Type O,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:41.060
Type AB, that has to do with carbohydrates that&nbsp;
are on the surface of red blood cells. And also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:46.730
if you've heard about being like A-positive or&nbsp;
O-negative, that positive and that negative is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:51.260
referring to Rh antigens, which are also just&nbsp;
PROTEINS (not carbohydrates) that are stuck on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.260 --> 00:05:00.440
red blood cells. And so what can actually happen&nbsp;
here is if the immune system, if these antibodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:07.640
recognize either the ABO or the Rh antigens on&nbsp;
red blood cells as being foreign, they start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:15.470
attack red blood cells and this is what can be&nbsp;
dangerous with blood transfusions. So hemolytic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:21.140
blood transfusions when someone gets a transfusion&nbsp;
of blood that is not sort of compatible with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:26.060
blood type, that is because these antibodies are&nbsp;
attacking these red blood cells that are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:31.640
put into the body from a foreign source that&nbsp;
aren't compatible. Also you may have heard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:39.170
hemolytic disease of the newborn. This is when a&nbsp;
mother's immune system actually starts to attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:48.110
the baby's blood when the baby is sort of still&nbsp;
in in utero or just afterwards because of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:56.750
sort of incompatibility where the mom's antibodies&nbsp;
start to attack, attack on recognizing the baby's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:05.720
different antigens. Now let's talk about type 3&nbsp;
hypersensitivities. Type 3 hypersensitivity are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:13.340
specifically characterized by these things called&nbsp;
immune complexes. Immune complexes are when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:22.790
have aggregations of antibody and soluble antigen&nbsp;
so not just one antibody recognizing one soluble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:28.020
antigen, but multiple antibodies recognizing&nbsp;
more than one soluble antigen at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:33.510
binding to more than one at a time. And then&nbsp;
those same soluble antigens will be bound by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:39.780
other antibodies and you just get this large mass&nbsp;
of all of these soluble antigens that are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:45.180
recognized by all of these antibodies at once and&nbsp;
that's called an immune complex. And the immune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:50.970
complex will deposit into blood vessel walls, and&nbsp;
once it's deposited into the blood vessel walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:57.810
it causes a couple of things that happen. First&nbsp;
of all, it causes complement cascades. Complement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:04.290
are types of proteins that are part of the&nbsp;
immune system that cause this cascade where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:09.180
all these complement proteins are activating&nbsp;
other complement proteins and they eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:17.130
lead to something called a membrane attack complex&nbsp;
where they form a hole in a cell and bust it open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:23.610
and kill it. And when this is happening, this is&nbsp;
happening in the body to host cells rather than to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:28.620
pathogens when complement cascade results in the&nbsp;
death of pathogen so it's a good thing, when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:34.500
result in the death of host cells like in type 3&nbsp;
hypersensitivity, that's a bad thing. Something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:39.240
else that happens when these immune complexes&nbsp;
deposit into blood vessel walls is neutrophil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:47.100
degranulation. We had previously mentioned&nbsp;
in type 1 hypersensitivities with mast cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:55.320
degranulating, that means they just spill forth&nbsp;
and sort of secrete all the components that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.320 --> 00:08:02.310
held inside their cells. And for neutrophils this&nbsp;
means a lot of sort of cytotoxic enzymes, a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:09.810
enzymes that are meant to degrade and break down&nbsp;
things, and so when complement starts to have its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:15.600
cascade and neutrophils degranulate, there's all&nbsp;
of this sort of damage that's being done, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:23.880
results in widespread inflammation in the body.&nbsp;
So lots of inflammation, lots of tissue damage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:29.760
an example of this is the autoimmune disorder&nbsp;
lupus, which you might have heard of before and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:36.000
lupus is where antibodies bind either to self&nbsp;
DNA or to self proteins. Remember when we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.110
self antigens being recognized antibodies, we call&nbsp;
that autoimmunity. So this is another place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:48.160
autoimmune disorders happen, specifically being&nbsp;
caused by these immune complexes, and causing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:53.530
sort of systemic diseases like lupus. Now let's&nbsp;
move on to the final type of hypersensitivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.530 --> 00:09:03.370
type 4. Type 4 is different than the first three&nbsp;
types in particular because it is cell-mediated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:15.220
It is actually regulated by T cells. So whereas&nbsp;
types 1 and 2 and 3 all involve antibodies doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:21.910
something that was going to harm the host, type&nbsp;
4 it's about T cells doing something wrong. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:30.760
the T cells can be Th1 or Th2 cells, different&nbsp;
kinds of T helper cells, it can also be cytotoxic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:35.650
lymphocytes, which is a different kind of T cell.&nbsp;
But either way, it's considered cell-mediated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:43.240
rather than antibody-mediated and it's also&nbsp;
known as a delayed type of hypersensitivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:53.350
so delayed hypersensitivity. And all this means is&nbsp;
that the T cells, they start to attack something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:59.530
that otherwise shouldn't be harmful in order to&nbsp;
get it out of the body. This results in things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:07.030
like latex allergies, poison ivy, the rash, the&nbsp;
dermatitis that you get from contact with poison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:12.460
ivy, and also transplant rejection. So if you hear&nbsp;
about someone who maybe got a lung transplant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:18.580
then their body rejected it, that is because&nbsp;
of a type 4 hypersensitivity where the T cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:24.820
recognized either the latex or the poison ivy&nbsp;
or the the lung transplant, one of these things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:31.000
and said "hey, that shouldn't be here" and they&nbsp;
have this really strong overreaction to eliminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:36.670
what they view as a threat, and it results in&nbsp;
either sort of an allergy or a rash or at worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:44.120
a major rejection of the needed organ transplant.&nbsp;
And known as a delayed hypersensitivity because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:51.200
can take several days sometimes for these things,&nbsp;
for the symptoms, to appear. So several days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:57.860
following exposure in some cases. And so that is&nbsp;
sort of it for the four types of hypersensitivity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:04.730
I have another video specifically on on&nbsp;
antibodies, on antibody structure and function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:09.050
and so if you're interested in learning more about&nbsp;
some of these antibodies, what they look like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:13.603
what they do, then please check out that video&nbsp;
and thanks for watching Biology Professor! :)

